.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.\" Copyright (C) 2026 Kevin H. Patterson
.Dd October 29, 2026
.Dt ZAR 1
.Os zar @PROJECT_VERSION@
.Sh NAME
.Nm zar
.Nd create a random-access, zstd-compressed archive
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar options
.Ar path
.Nm
.Op Ar options
.Fl m
.Fl o Ar archive
.Ar path ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
archives a file or directory tree.
Files are compressed with zstd in independently checksummed chunks, so
.Xr unzar 1
can list, verify, or extract any file without decompressing the rest.
Directories, symbolic links, and hard links are stored as such, with
permissions and modification times.
A chunk that lies entirely within a hole of a sparse file is recorded as a
hole, without being read or stored, and
.Xr unzar 1
recreates it as a hole.
Devices, FIFOs, and sockets are skipped with a warning.
.Pp
A
.Ar path
is stored under its own name, so extracting the archive recreates it:
.Bl -tag -width "proj/." -offset indent
.It Pa proj
the file, directory, or symbolic link (not followed)
.Pa proj ,
stored as
.Pa proj
.It Pa proj/
the same, but
.Pa proj
must be a directory; a symbolic link to one is followed
.It Pa proj/.
the contents of
.Pa proj ,
without
.Pa proj
itself
.It Pa \&.
the contents of the current directory
.El
.Pp
Without
.Fl o ,
the archive is written to
.Ar name Ns Pa .zar
in the current directory, where
.Ar name
is the last component of
.Ar path
(for
.Pa \&. ,
the current directory's name).
The archive is written to a temporary file and renamed into place only when
complete, so a failed run leaves nothing behind.
An archive being written, or being replaced, is never included in itself.
.Pp
Duplicate chunks are stored once, and files whose names suggest they are
already compressed (JPEG, MP4, ZIP, and so on) are stored without
compression.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o , Fl \-output Ns = Ns Ar file
Write the archive to
.Ar file ,
or to standard output if
.Ar file
is
.Ql - .
.Nm
refuses to write an archive to a terminal.
.It Fl m , Fl \-many
Archive several
.Ar path
arguments into one archive.
Requires
.Fl o .
Two paths that would be stored under the same name are an error.
.It Fl y , Fl \-yes
Overwrite an existing archive.
.It Fl l , Fl \-level Ns = Ns Ar n
The zstd compression level, from the library's minimum (negative, fastest)
to its maximum (22).
The default is 3.
.It Fl j , Fl \-threads Ns Op = Ns Ar n
Use
.Ar n
worker threads, from 1 to 1024.
The default, and the value without
.Ar n ,
is one per CPU core.
.It Fl \-chunk-size Ns = Ns Ar size
Split files into independently compressed chunks of
.Ar size
bytes: a power of two from 4K to 1G.
The default is 1M.
Holes in sparse files are kept a chunk at a time, so smaller chunks keep
smaller holes.
.It Fl \-align Ns Op = Ns Ar n
Align each block's payload to
.Ar n
bytes, a power of two up to 1M.
The default is 8; without
.Ar n ,
the CPU's cache-line size.
.It Fl \-owner Ns Op = Ns Ar which
Store file ownership:
.Cm ids ,
.Cm names ,
or
.Cm both
(the default without
.Ar which ) .
By default ownership isn't stored.
.It Fl \-nsec
Store timestamps to the nanosecond, not just the second.
.It Fl \-all-times
Store access, change, and (where the system records it) birth times as
well as modification times.
.It Fl \-names Ns = Ns Ar mode
What to do with file names that aren't valid UTF-8:
.Cm raw
(the default) stores their bytes as they are,
.Cm skip
leaves those files out, and
.Cm latin1
converts them from ISO 8859-1.
Each is reported.
.It Fl \-compress-all
Compress every file, even those whose names suggest they are already
compressed.
.It Fl \-dedup Ns = Ns Cm on Ns | Ns Cm off
Store identical chunks only once.
This is on by default whenever the output can be read back, which excludes
a pipe unless
.Fl \-head-index
is given;
.Cm on
makes that an error instead.
.It Fl \-contiguous Ns Op = Ns Ar size
Store each file of at most
.Ar size
bytes as a single block rather than in chunks (without
.Ar size ,
files up to 4G).
With
.Fl \-align ,
this lets a file be read in place.
.It Fl \-dict Ns Op = Ns Ar size
Train a zstd dictionary of at most
.Ar size
bytes (default 110K) on the files and compress them with it.
This helps most with many small, similar files.
.It Fl \-head-index
Also write the index at the front of the archive, so that
.Xr unzar 1
can extract it from a pipe.
The index is written twice.
.It Fl \-solid Ns = Ns Ar size
First try compressing the whole archive as a single zstd stream, and keep
that if it is at most
.Ar size
bytes.
A solid archive compresses many small files better, but can't be read at
random: listing or extracting one file decompresses everything before it.
Otherwise a normal archive is written, and
.Fl \-align
applies only then.
.It Fl \-dict-over-solid Ns = Ns Ar percent
When the solid archive fits, also try a normal archive compressed with a
dictionary, and keep it instead if it is at most
.Ar percent
of the solid archive's size (and fits too).
The default is 120; 0 never tries.
.It Fl v , Fl \-verbose
List each path on standard error as it is added, and print a summary at the
end, including how much data was deduplicated or in holes.
.It Fl \-help
Show a summary of the options.
.It Fl \-version
Show the version, the archive format version, and the zstd version.
.El
.Pp
Short options can be combined, as in
.Fl myv .
The level for
.Fl l
can be attached or separate
.Pq Fl l19 No or Fl l No 19 ,
but the count for
.Fl j
must be attached, as in
.Fl j4 .
Sizes take an optional
.Cm K ,
.Cm M ,
.Cm G ,
or
.Cm T
suffix, for binary multiples.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev TMPDIR
Where temporary files go when writing to standard output (with
.Fl \-head-index
or
.Fl \-solid ) .
Otherwise they go beside the archive.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
The archive was written, but some files had problems, each reported on
standard error.
.It 2
A fatal error: bad usage, a missing input, an existing archive without
.Fl y ,
and so on.
No archive is left behind.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Archive
.Pa proj
as
.Pa proj.zar :
.Dl $ zar proj
.Pp
Archive the current directory's contents, more slowly but smaller:
.Dl $ zar -l 19 -o ../site.zar .
.Pp
Archive two trees into one archive:
.Dl $ zar -m -o both.zar src docs
.Pp
Send an archive through a pipe to another machine and extract it there:
.Dl $ zar --head-index -o - proj | ssh host unzar -C /srv -
.Pp
Package many small files as compactly as possible, if it comes to at most
1 MiB:
.Dl $ zar --solid=1M config
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr unzar 1 ,
.Xr zstd 1
.Pp
The archive format is specified in
.Pa FORMAT.md ,
installed with the documentation.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Kevin H. Patterson
